<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Drunken Sailor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">sung by the 1830s; first described in print from a whaling voyage of <a href="/years/1839">1839</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Drunken Sailor&quot;, also known as &quot;What Shall We Do with a Drunken Sailor?&quot; or &quot;Up She Rises&quot;, is a traditional sea shanty, number 322 in the Roud Folk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> Index. Who made it and where is not known. It resembles the traditional Irish song &quot;Óró sé do bheatha abhaile&quot; in its chord progression and in its repeated words over melodic sequences, and it is in the Dorian mode.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>&quot;<b>Drunken Sailor</b>&quot;, also known as &quot;What Shall We Do with a Drunken Sailor?&quot; or &quot;Up She Rises&quot;, is a traditional sea shanty, number 322 in the Roud Folk Song Index.</blockquote><a href="/songs/drunken-sailor">Drunken Sailor</a></li></ul></section>
